{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>כברת הארץ.</b> Menachem explained the term כַּבִּיר — a multitudinous, great distance. And its aggadah: at the time when the earth is hollow and perforated like a sieve (Bereshit Rabbah) — when lamp-light is available, winter has passed and the heat has not yet come (ibid.) — but this is not the plain sense of Scripture, for in the case of Naaman we find ״וַיֵּלֶךְ מֵאִתּוֹ כִּבְרַת אֶרֶץ״ (II Kings 5). And I say that it is a term for a land-measurement, like a parasang's distance or more — just as you say ״צֶמֶד כֶּרֶם״ (Isaiah 5), ״חֶלְקַת הַשָּׂדֶה״ (Genesis 33) — so too for a person's travel distance one gives the measure-name כִּבְרַת אֶרֶץ."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:11.808957581Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}